# PART 9 – REAL-TIME AUCTION ENGINE

## 16. Live bidding system

This is the most important technical feature of the entire application.

Build a genuine server-authoritative real-time bidding system.

Every team must be able to place bids simultaneously.

All connected devices must receive the latest accepted auction state.

The following must update in real time:

- Current highest bid.
- Current highest-bidding team.
- Bid history.
- Auction countdown.
- Auction status.
- Winning team announcement.

Updates must appear on:
- Host Dashboard.
- All Team Dashboards.
- Audience Display.

Use room-specific real-time communication.

Do not refresh the entire page to display new bids.

## 17. Bid validation rules

The backend must validate every bid.

A bid is valid only when:

1. The participant is authenticated or has a valid team session.
2. The team belongs to the active room.
3. The auction is currently open.
4. The auction deadline has not passed.
5. The bid is a valid positive number.
6. The bid satisfies the starting-bid requirement.
7. The bid is higher than the current highest bid by at least the configured increment.
8. The team is not attempting to outbid itself.
9. The bid request is not a duplicate.
10. The bid is accepted by the backend.

Reject invalid bids with clear messages.

## 18. Simultaneous bidding and priority

If two or more teams press the bid button at nearly the same time, the backend must determine the order of accepted bids.

Implement:

- Server-generated timestamps.
- Unique bid IDs.
- Monotonically increasing server-side sequence numbers per auction.
- Atomic database transactions or equivalent concurrency control.
- Server-side validation against the latest accepted bid.

The first valid bid successfully committed by the server gets priority.

If a later bid is valid and sufficiently higher, it can become the new highest bid.

If two requests attempt the same bid amount, process them in server transaction order. The first accepted request is recorded; the next request must be checked against the new highest bid and rejected if it no longer meets the minimum increment.

Never use the participant's phone clock to decide bid priority.

Never rely only on frontend validation.

Never allow two teams to simultaneously become the highest bidder.

Maintain an immutable bid history containing:
- Bid ID.
- Team ID.
- Team name.
- Bid amount.
- Server timestamp.
- Sequence number.
- Acceptance/rejection status.

## 19. Auction countdown

Use a server-authoritative countdown.

When the host starts an auction:
- Record the server start timestamp.
- Record the server deadline timestamp.
- Broadcast the deadline to every connected client.

Each client calculates the displayed remaining time using the authoritative deadline.

Do not use separate independent timers on different devices as the source of truth.

When the deadline is reached:
- Automatically close bidding on the backend.
- Reject late bid requests.
- Determine the highest accepted bid.
- Identify the winning team.
- Broadcast the auction result.

Support page refresh and reconnection without resetting the timer.

If the host refreshes the page, the current auction must resume from the stored state.

